C++17 constexpr visitor on `std::variant<int, float, std::string>` compiles to a jump table labelled by `std::visit` overload resolution.

Example:
```cpp
std::variant<int, float, std::string> v = 42;
std::visit(overloaded {
    [](int i) { cout<<"int "<<i; },
    [](float f) { cout<<"float "<<f; },
    [](const string& s) { cout<<"string "<<s; }
}, v);
```

Ghidra decompiler output (typical x86-64 clang 15 -O2):
```
void visitor_dispatcher(undefined8 param_1, variant *v) {
    switch(v->index) {
    case 0:   // int
        (*(code *)param_1)(*(int *)(v->data + 0x10));
        return;
    case 1:   // float
        (*(code *)param_1)(*(float *)(v->data + 0x10));
        return;
    case 2:   // string
        (*(code *)param_1)(v->data + 0x10);
        return;
    default:
        __assert_fail("variant index out of range","visitor.cpp",143,"main");
    }
}
```

How to reconstruct the dispatcher statically:
1. Identify `v->index` field via struct offset:
   • Search references to `variant::index` → Ghidra `vtable` shows member at offset 0x8.
2. Trace back to `std::visit` call via xref → find lambda array (`.rodata` offset 0x4040a0).
3. Reconstruct lambda signature via signature matching:
   • `int case0(void*, int)` → signature `int(int)`.
   • `float case1(void*, float)` → signature `int(float)`.
4. Build Ghidra Python script:
```python
index = get_bytes(variant_struct, 0x8, 4)  # read v->index
lambdas = [0x4040a0, 0x4040b0, 0x4040c0]
target = lambdas[index]
createFunction(target, "visitor_case"+str(index))
```

Mitigations against reverse engineering:
• Enable Obfuscator-LLVM `-mllvm -sub_loop=3` to flatten visitor jump table.
• Use `-fvisibility=hidden` to hide lambda vtables.

